# Carom — Debug and automation surface

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Carom carries a small debugging and automation surface, `window.__carom`, so the
game can be driven and inspected from code without touching the keyboard or
waiting on real time.

You implement it. Every operation this file specifies is a deliverable, and the
build installs the finished surface on `window.__carom` as soon as the game has
initialized. Every scenario driven from code reaches the game through it, so it
is present and exactly as specified here.

Each operation is a read or a pose of the game's state. It arranges the world,
and the game's own update runs the real collision, the real serve, and the real
AI from there on the next frame, so a scenario driven from code behaves exactly
like one played by hand.

The surface is inert during normal play, doing nothing until something calls it.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Carom carries a small debugging and automation surface so the game can be driven
and inspected from code without touching the keyboard or waiting on real time.

You implement it. Every operation this file specifies is a deliverable, and the
build's `initialize` returns the finished surface beside the state it built, as
the pair `[state, debug]`. The engine returns that same value from
`engine.debug`, and it is reached that way alone: nothing is installed on the
page. Every scenario driven from code reaches the game through it, so it is
present and exactly as specified here.

Each operation is a reading or a pose of `CaromState`, written in the shape of
`update`, because the engine holds the state by value and nothing holds a
writable one. A pose takes the current state, as `DeepReadonly<CaromState>`,
and returns the next `CaromState`; a reading takes the state the same way and
returns what it read. A caller drives a pose through the engine's `apply`, as
`engine.apply((s) => debug.serve(s))`, and a reading against the engine's
current state, as `debug.snapshot(engine.state)`. A pose arranges the world, and
the game's own `update` runs the real collision, the real serve, and the real AI
from there on the next frame, so a scenario driven from code behaves exactly like
one played by hand. Where the surface's implementation lives under `src/` is
yours to decide; `src/game.ts` declares and exports its type, `CaromDebugApi`.

The surface is inert during normal play, doing nothing until something calls it.
{{else}}
Carom carries a small debugging and automation surface so the game can be driven
and inspected from code without touching the keyboard or waiting on real time.

You implement it. Every operation this file specifies is a deliverable, and the
game instance's `initialize` builds the finished surface and returns it. The
engine hands that same object back from `engine.debug`, and it is reached that
way alone: nothing is installed on the page. Every scenario driven from code
reaches the game through it, so it is present and exactly as specified here.

Each operation is a method that acts on the running game. A pose takes only the
arguments its heading names, returns nothing, and arranges the live world
through the same systems play uses: it drives the game mode, spawns or moves the
actors, and leaves the outcome to the frames that follow, so the game's own
collision, the real serve, and the real AI produce the result and a scenario
driven from code behaves exactly like one played by hand. A reading takes no
arguments and returns plain data, read off the world at the instant of the call.
Where the surface's implementation lives under `src/` is yours to decide; the
type your instance declares for it, the `D` of its `GameInstance<D>`, is the
surface this file specifies.

The surface is inert during normal play, doing nothing until something calls it.
{{/if}}

## A deterministic core

The surface rests on the simulation being deterministic: every rate integrated
against the delta time the game is given, so an interval of game time reaches the
same state however it was divided into frames. Two more properties make it
driveable from code:

- Render-free core. Game state advances from the elapsed time the game is handed,
  independent of a canvas, of the frame loop that measured it, and of wall-clock
  time. The dependency runs one way: the simulation reads nothing from the
  renderer.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
- Seeded randomness. Any randomness the game uses runs off a generator seeded from
  `state.rngState`, and it keeps its whole generator state in that field, so
  reseeding and replaying the same calls reproduces the same result exactly. A
  build that uses no randomness simply never reads it.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
- Seeded randomness. Any randomness the game uses runs off a generator seeded from
  `state.rngState`, and it keeps its whole generator state in that field, so
  reseeding and replaying the same calls reproduces the same result exactly. A
  build that uses no randomness simply never reads it.
{{else}}
- Seeded randomness. Any randomness the game uses runs off a generator the
  surface's `reset` seeds, and the game keeps that generator's whole state, so
  reseeding and replaying the same calls reproduces the same result exactly. A
  build that uses no randomness simply never draws from it.
{{/if}}

Given the same seed and the same sequence of calls and elapsed game time, the game
reaches the same state every time.

## What the runtime provides instead

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The keyboard and the overlay belong to the runtime layer beneath the game, and
the surface carries no operation for either. That layer delivers the actions the
game registers from the key bindings this specification fixes, and it draws and
toggles the debug overlay.

The clock is the exception, because nothing outside this build owns it. The
surface is what takes the game off real time and advances it, through
`setAutoStep` and `advance` below.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
The clock, the keyboard, and the overlay belong to the {{engine.name}} engine,
which documents them in the `engine/` directory, and the surface carries no
operation for any of them. The engine advances the game frame by frame and can
take it off real time, it drives the actions the game registers, and it draws and
toggles the debug overlay.
{{else}}
The clock, the keyboard, and the overlay belong to the {{engine.name}} engine,
which documents them in the `engine/` directory, and the surface carries no
operation for any of them. The engine advances the game frame by frame and a
scripted clock takes it off real time, it drives the actions the game registers,
and it draws and toggles the debug overlay.
{{/if}}

What the overlay shows is Carom's, under Diagnostics below.

## The driver

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
`state.driver` is how the surface takes the paddles from the player, and honoring
it is the one behavior the whole surface depends on.

- While `driver.paddles` is `false`, the game plays normally: the registered
  actions move the human paddles and, in Solo, the AI moves the right one.
- Every operation that poses the game sets `driver.paddles` to `true`. From then
  on both paddles move at the held velocities `driver.vy.left` and
  `driver.vy.right`, in units per second, integrated against the frame's delta
  time and clamped to `[PADDLE_MIN_CY, PADDLE_MAX_CY]` exactly as a played paddle
  is. The input actions and the AI leave the paddles alone while it is `true`.
- `snapshot` and `reset` leave the driver as they found it.
- `setAutoStep` and `advance` leave it alone as well: moving the clock is not
  posing the world.
- The exception is `driver.ai`. In Solo, while `driver.paddles` and `driver.ai`
  are both `true`, the right paddle is driven by the real AI opponent while the
  left paddle stays on its held velocity.
- `reset` sets `driver.paddles` and `driver.ai` back to `false` and both held
  velocities to `0`, handing the paddles back.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
`state.driver` is how the surface takes the paddles from the player, and honoring
it is the one behavior the whole surface depends on.

- While `driver.paddles` is `false`, the game plays normally: the registered
  actions move the human paddles and, in Solo, the AI moves the right one.
- Every operation that poses the game sets `driver.paddles` to `true`. From then
  on both paddles move at the held velocities `driver.vy.left` and
  `driver.vy.right`, in units per second, integrated against the frame's delta
  time and clamped to `[PADDLE_MIN_CY, PADDLE_MAX_CY]` exactly as a played paddle
  is. The input actions and the AI leave the paddles alone while it is `true`.
- `snapshot` and `reset` leave the driver as they found it.
- The exception is `driver.ai`. In Solo, while `driver.paddles` and `driver.ai`
  are both `true`, the right paddle is driven by the real AI opponent while the
  left paddle stays on its held velocity.
- `reset` sets `driver.paddles` and `driver.ai` back to `false` and both held
  velocities to `0`, handing the paddles back.
{{else}}
The driver is the hold the surface takes on the paddles, and honoring it is the
one behavior the whole surface depends on. How the build carries it is its own —
a controller of the build's that possesses both paddles is the natural shape —
but its behavior is fixed:

- While the driver holds nothing, the game plays normally: the registered
  actions move the human paddles and, in Solo, the AI moves the right one.
- Every operation that poses the game takes both paddles into the driver's hold.
  From then on each paddle moves at the velocity the driver holds for its side,
  in units per second, integrated against the frame's delta time and clamped to
  `[PADDLE_MIN_CY, PADDLE_MAX_CY]` exactly as a played paddle is. The input
  actions and the AI leave the paddles alone while the hold lasts.
- `snapshot` leaves the driver as it found it.
- The exception is the AI switch `setAiControl` flips. In Solo, while the driver
  holds the paddles with that switch on, the right paddle is driven by the real
  AI opponent while the left paddle stays on its held velocity.
- `reset` releases the hold, sets both held velocities to `0`, and turns the AI
  switch off, handing the paddles back.
{{/if}}

A paddle's `vy` field reports its actual vertical velocity for the frame, whoever
moved it, because that is what the spin mechanic reads at contact. A paddle pinned
against a bound therefore reports `0` while a movement action is held or a driver
velocity is set.

## The operations

The surface carries `version` (`CAROM_DEBUG_VERSION`, `1`), a plain number, and
the operations below. Values are plain numbers, strings, and booleans;
coordinates and velocities are in the field's logical units.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}

Each operation's first parameter is the current state, `DeepReadonly<CaromState>`.
A pose returns the next `CaromState`, built from the one it was handed, which it
leaves as it was; a reading returns what it read. The headings below name the
parameters that follow the state.
{{else}}

A pose takes only the parameters its heading names and returns nothing; a
reading takes none and returns what it read. Each operation acts on the live
game at the moment of the call.
{{/if}}

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `setAutoStep(enabled)`

Takes the game off real time, and gives it back. `setAutoStep(false)` stops the
frame loop advancing the simulation from the wall clock, so the game changes only
when `advance` says so. `setAutoStep(true)` returns it to running itself, which is
how a build starts and how it is played.

Drawing is unaffected either way: the loop keeps rendering, so the canvas shows
the state the most recent frame left.

### `advance(seconds, frames)`

Runs `frames` whole frames covering `seconds` of game time, each worth
`seconds / frames`, immediately and in order. `frames` defaults to `1`. Each is a
real frame — the same update the loop runs, then a render — so the game's own
collision, serve and AI produce the result and the canvas reflects it.

Every rate in this game is per second and integrated against the frame's delta
time, so `advance(1, 1)` and `advance(1, 60)` cover the same second of game time
and must reach the same outcome, beyond the drift a change in step size explains.

Advancing while the game is still stepping automatically adds to what the wall
clock is already doing, so a scenario that must be reproducible calls
`setAutoStep(false)` first.

{{/if}}
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
